# p014 (29-mark Golomb ruler, baseline 623) — feasibility probe, 2026-11-04
# local macOS, single thread, gcc -O3

## tool
src/scnet/p014/golomb.c — simulated annealing on a fixed length L.
  marks m[0]=0 < ... < m[28]=L, cost = number of COLLIDING DIFFERENCE PAIRS
  (cost 0 <=> valid ruler). Move = relocate one mark to any free position;
  delta is exact (remove all old differences, then add all new, then undo) and was
  verified against full recomputation on 2,000 random states ("DELTA_OK").

## control: the baseline ruler itself
L=623, seeded with the Singer ruler -> cost 0 immediately (sanity check passes).

## warm start: L=622, the baseline compressed to [0,622]
start cost = 14 (marks 0 2 27 32 40 94 155 157 192 206 232 251 266 337 338 354
                    393 402 420 472 478 501 522 569 579 591 611 615 622)
T = 0.2 : 4,030 accepted moves in 25 s, best stays 14
T = 0.5 : 5,122 accepted moves in 25 s, best stays 14
T = 1.0 : 2.0e6 moves, cost wanders 60-80, best stays 14
T = 2.0 : 4.0e6 moves, cost wanders 70-90, best stays 14
T = 3.0 : 8.0e6 moves, cost wanders 70-105, best stays 14
=> the compressed ruler is a deep local minimum at L=622; no sampled move improves it.

## how strong is the solver at all?
random start, L=623 (a ruler provably exists): 133 -> 31 in 60 s
random start, L=622:                          178 -> 30 in 30 s
=> even where a solution exists, this annealer is far from reaching cost 0.

## reading
The probe does not show that 622 is impossible; it shows that a generic annealer is
one to two orders of magnitude too weak to attack this instance. Rokicki-Dogon report
that the finite-geometry constructions give the best known rulers for every n > 16,
i.e. no heuristic has beaten 623 for 29 marks either.
A serious attempt would need a dedicated solver (difference-triangle tabu with
+-1 shift moves and plateau handling, or a CP/SAT model with propagation), not this.

## second round — two stronger methods, same verdict

### CP-SAT (OR-Tools 9.15, `src/scnet/p014/cpsat_probe.py`)
model: p_0=0 < p_1 < ... < p_28=L, AllDifferent over all 406 differences.
  L=623 **with** the baseline as hint : 0.1 s, trivially reproduces it (hint = solution).
  L=623 **without** hint, 8 workers    : UNKNOWN after 60 s — it cannot even find a
                                          ruler that is known to exist.
=> all-different propagation on 406 differences is far too weak; CP-SAT is not the tool.

### +-1 tabu with plateau moves (`src/scnet/p014/golomb2.c`)
cost as before; each iteration takes the best of all 2*27 single-step shifts
(+1/-1 on one mark), plateau moves allowed.
  L=623 seeded with the baseline : cost 0 immediately (control).
  L=622 seeded with the compressed baseline : 16,729,446 iterations in 60 s,
      cost stays EXACTLY 14 the whole time, best never improves.
=> the compressed ruler is also a strict local minimum in the +-1 neighbourhood, and
   plateau walking does not escape it.

### combined reading (three independent methods)
generic SA, CP-SAT, and +-1 tabu all fail on the same instance. The warm start at
L=622 has 14 colliding difference pairs and nothing moves it. The problem statement
adds that a reviewer already scanned every unit and every window of the Singer and
Bose constructions for q=29,31,32 and found nothing shorter than 623.
This is consistent with Rokicki-Dogon's Hypothesis A (for n>16 the finite-geometry
constructions are optimal). It is still not a proof that 622 is impossible, but the
probability that 20 h of SCNet quota finds one is very low.
